  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 138 PM MDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThe long term period is shaping up to be unseasonably warm for the \ncounty warning area (CWA). A persistent weak upper-level ridge is \nforecast to remain over the region Saturday through early next \nweek. High temperatures are forecast to be in the high 90s to \n100s Saturday through Monday. Tuesday is looking slightly cooler\nbut high temperatures are still forecast to be in the 90s for \nthe CWA. \n\nFire weather potential will be something to monitor for throughout \nthe week. Currently, fuels are dry throughout the entire CWA. \nRelative humidity (RH) values are forecast in the low 20s for the \nwestern CWA Saturday and slowly creep down to the mid to high teens \nthrough Monday. Afternoon wind gusts up to 30 mph are possible for \nthe western CWA, but lowest RH values and strongest wind gusts are \nnot forecast to coincide. A few hours of critical fire weather \nconditions could still be possible given the dry fuels and \nunseasonably warm temperatures. \n\nTuesday, a ridge building west of the Rocky Mountain will help \ntransition our region to more of a northwest upper-level flow. We \ncould see some showers and thunderstorms late Tuesday into Wednesday \ndue to the increased upper-level support from a weak jet streak \noverhead. Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) are around 30-40% for \nthe CWA. Models are in disagreement on where and when the ridge west \nof the Rocky Mountains will set up after Tuesday. If it ends up \nsetting up over the Tri-State area, we could have another round of \nunseasonably warm temperatures and dry conditions.",
